import { BytesReaderCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> BytesReaderCopy = BytesReaderCopy {
  summary: "hex.decode checks a complete hexadecimal string and returns either successful Bytes or a HexDecodeError. It accepts uppercase but does not accept 0x or whitespace.",
  taskTitle: "Validate pasted hexadecimal text",
  purpose: "Suppose a text field supplies 00FF and you want to turn it into bytes before using it. Check the entire input as hexadecimal instead of accepting a prefix that happened to decode.",
  typescriptBefore: "TypeScript’s Uint8Array.fromHex(input) reads hexadecimal and throws on invalid input. readHex uses try/catch to separate success from rejection, then writes successful bytes in lowercase with toHex(). This compares acceptance of the same inputs, rather than the classification of each failure.",
  benefit: "Seseragi includes failure in the returned value. decode returns Either<HexDecodeError, Bytes>: Left carries a failure reason and Right carries successful Bytes. The Right branch makes the place where valid bytes are used explicit.",
  setup: "hex is the alias assigned to std/bytes/hex by import * as. decode reads a string, and encode turns bytes into hexadecimal for display. Bytes is an immutable sequence of byte values from 0 through 255.",
  after: [
    "fn read input: String -> String defines a function that receives a string named input and returns a display string. fn starts the definition, : String gives the argument type, and -> String gives the return type. hex.decode input calls the function with a space before its argument.",
    "match selects a branch according to whether Left or Right arrived. The _ in Left _ means the detailed reason is not used here. Right content names the successful Bytes content. + joins Accepted:  to the string from hex.encode content.",
    "00FF is accepted and displayed as Accepted: 00ff. The empty string is also accepted as empty Bytes. The remaining inputs have a missing digit, invalid characters, a prefix, or whitespace, so they produce Rejected. println (read input) prints the result of read.",
    "The content in Right content is bytes, not text. To use those bytes as text, separately call decodeUtf8 from std/text and take the String from its successful result.",
  ],
  details: [
    "Accepted digits are ASCII 0–9, a–f, and A–F. Two digits represent one byte, and uppercase and lowercase may be mixed. The decoder does not automatically remove 0x or separators.",
    "The first check asks whether the input’s length in UTF-8 bytes is even. An odd length becomes the value carried by OddHexLength. If the subsequent digit check fails, InvalidHexDigit carries an offset counting byte positions from zero.",
    "é0 occupies three UTF-8 bytes, so OddHexLength carries 3. é occupies two bytes, passes the length check, and produces InvalidHexDigit at offset 0. Do not substitute JavaScript String.length or the number of visible characters for that byte length.",
    "Failure returns no successful prefix. To correct a mistaken input such as 0x00, check the sender’s format and handle an allowed prefix separately if appropriate. Successfully reading the representation does not validate the meaning or safety of its contents.",
  ],
  nodeNote: "The inspected Node 24 runtime has no fromHex()/toHex(). For Node/Bun’s Buffer, add import { Buffer } from \"node:buffer\". Buffer.from(input, \"hex\") may truncate at invalid input, so validate even length and ASCII hexadecimal digits first. This can match the acceptance policy, but it does not reproduce Seseragi’s error categories or check order.",
}
